WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:02.325
<v Examiner>Exam case 8.

2
00:00:02.325 --> 00:00:03.927
<v Examiner>Joints and arthritis.

3
00:00:04.377 --> 00:00:07.987
<v Examiner>Shannon is thirty, and has had a series of joint problems.

4
00:00:07.987 --> 00:00:10.601
<v Examiner>She's had bursitis in her hip in the past,

5
00:00:10.601 --> 00:00:14.708
<v Examiner>and now has pain mainly in her knee and hip joints, and her hands.

6
00:00:14.708 --> 00:00:18.317
<v Examiner>She has recently been diagnosed with rheumatoid arthritis.

7
00:00:18.317 --> 00:00:21.180
<v Examiner>She's concerned she's too young for arthritis,

8
00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:25.723
<v Examiner>even though she's fairly active and has been an athlete most of her life.

9
00:00:25.723 --> 00:00:31.324
<v Examiner>Part of her treatment plan is prednisone, a synthetic corticosteroid, similar to cortisol.

10
00:00:31.324 --> 00:00:34.871
<v Examiner>Cortisol is part of the body's long-term stress response.

11
00:00:35.321 --> 00:00:38.606
Case 8 is two tracks, and this one is the joint itself.

12
00:00:38.606 --> 00:00:43.562
She was clear in her session: the diagrams in the case studies will be in the exam,

13
00:00:43.562 --> 00:00:44.995
and she's promising you.

14
00:00:44.995 --> 00:00:49.593
This joint is definitely in, though it might be numbered instead of lettered.

15
00:00:50.043 --> 00:00:50.669
<v Examiner>Nine marks.

16
00:00:50.669 --> 00:00:55.675
<v Examiner>Use the diagram to name the lettered structures of the joint, with the function of each.

17
00:00:56.125 --> 00:00:58.835
Nine structures, a name and a job each.

18
00:00:58.835 --> 00:01:04.464
The hook, in letter order: muscle, enthesis, fluid, bursa, capsule, tendon, bone,

19
00:01:04.464 --> 00:01:05.853
cartilage, ligament.

20
00:01:06.303 --> 00:01:08.218
Letter A, top left, is muscle.

21
00:01:08.218 --> 00:01:13.578
It contracts and acts on the bones, making the angle between them smaller or larger.

22
00:01:13.578 --> 00:01:18.555
Her answer deck letters the muscle as I, but the workbook figure letters it A.

23
00:01:18.555 --> 00:01:20.661
Go by the figure in front of you.

24
00:01:21.111 --> 00:01:25.214
The other two A labels, top right and at the bottom, mark the enthesis:

25
00:01:25.214 --> 00:01:27.468
where the ligaments attach to the bone.

26
00:01:27.918 --> 00:01:29.373
B is synovial fluid.

27
00:01:29.373 --> 00:01:31.118
It lubricates the joint.

28
00:01:31.568 --> 00:01:32.459
C is a bursa.

29
00:01:32.459 --> 00:01:38.352
It reduces friction: it's a lubricated, fluid-filled sac between bone and soft tissue.

30
00:01:38.802 --> 00:01:40.236
D is the joint capsule.

31
00:01:40.236 --> 00:01:41.981
It holds the bones together.

32
00:01:43.431 --> 00:01:44.405
E is a tendon.

33
00:01:44.405 --> 00:01:46.076
It joins muscle to bone.

34
00:01:46.526 --> 00:01:47.208
F is bone.

35
00:01:47.208 --> 00:01:50.345
It acts as a lever, and articulates at joints.

36
00:01:50.795 --> 00:01:52.385
G is hyaline cartilage.

37
00:01:52.385 --> 00:01:55.083
It reduces friction, and absorbs shock.

38
00:01:55.533 --> 00:01:56.582
H is a ligament.

39
00:01:56.582 --> 00:01:58.157
It holds bones together.

40
00:01:59.607 --> 00:02:02.587
Her formative test drills the same diagram.

41
00:02:02.587 --> 00:02:06.677
B is synovial fluid, responsible for lubricating the joint.

42
00:02:06.677 --> 00:02:12.153
G is hyaline cartilage, and it is not responsible for secreting synovial fluid.

43
00:02:12.153 --> 00:02:15.896
The part arthritis mainly affects is G, the cartilage.

44
00:02:15.896 --> 00:02:21.303
And osteoarthritis occurs most often in the elderly, because of wear and tear.

45
00:02:21.753 --> 00:02:22.383
<v Examiner>Two marks.

46
00:02:22.383 --> 00:02:25.028
<v Examiner>The knee and hip are both synovial joints.

47
00:02:25.028 --> 00:02:28.430
<v Examiner>What are the benefits to the body of a synovial joint?

48
00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:31.071
Synovial joints are freely movable.

49
00:02:31.071 --> 00:02:34.640
They're the most freely moving type of joint in the body.

50
00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:37.520
Without them, we'd have very limited movement.

51
00:02:37.970 --> 00:02:38.544
<v Examiner>Two marks.

52
00:02:38.544 --> 00:02:41.584
<v Examiner>What kind of synovial joint are the hip and the knee?

53
00:02:41.584 --> 00:02:43.133
<v Examiner>The shoulder and the elbow?

54
00:02:43.583 --> 00:02:48.493
The shoulder and the hip are ball-and-socket joints: a ball moving in a socket.

55
00:02:48.493 --> 00:02:53.652
The knee and the elbow are hinge joints: they can only open and close, like a door.

56
00:02:55.102 --> 00:02:55.774
<v Examiner>Two marks.

57
00:02:55.774 --> 00:02:56.915
<v Examiner>What is bursitis?

58
00:02:57.365 --> 00:02:59.353
A bursa is a thin, lubricated,

59
00:02:59.353 --> 00:03:05.117
fluid-filled sac between a bone and the soft tissue around it, the tendons and muscles.

60
00:03:05.117 --> 00:03:07.370
It's a cushion to reduce friction.

61
00:03:07.370 --> 00:03:10.351
In bursitis, it becomes inflamed and swollen.

62
00:03:10.351 --> 00:03:13.664
Then it has the opposite effect, and it's painful.

63
00:03:14.114 --> 00:03:16.676
<v Examiner>And one she hasn't answered anywhere yet.

64
00:03:16.676 --> 00:03:19.426
<v Examiner>Compare and contrast bursitis and arthritis.

65
00:03:19.876 --> 00:03:24.586
Her answer isn't posted, so this is built from her two separate answers.

66
00:03:24.586 --> 00:03:29.689
What they share: both are inflammation, with pain and swelling around a joint,

67
00:03:29.689 --> 00:03:31.521
and both can limit movement.

68
00:03:31.521 --> 00:03:32.698
Where they differ.

69
00:03:32.698 --> 00:03:37.670
Bursitis is in the bursa, a fluid sac outside the joint, often from overuse,

70
00:03:37.670 --> 00:03:40.483
pressure or injury, and it usually settles.

71
00:03:40.483 --> 00:03:42.642
Arthritis is in the joint itself.

72
00:03:42.642 --> 00:03:48.137
In her words, arthritis wears away the articular cartilage and inflames the synovial

73
00:03:48.137 --> 00:03:48.726
membrane.

74
00:03:48.726 --> 00:03:50.492
And it's usually long-term.

75
00:03:50.492 --> 00:03:55.661
Shannon has had both: bursitis in her hip before, and rheumatoid arthritis now.

76
00:03:56.111 --> 00:03:56.740
<v Examiner>Once more.

77
00:03:56.740 --> 00:03:57.369
<v Examiner>The joint.

78
00:03:59.819 --> 00:04:04.038
The joint, letter by letter: muscle contracts to move the bones;

79
00:04:04.038 --> 00:04:08.388
the enthesis is where ligaments attach; synovial fluid lubricates;

80
00:04:08.388 --> 00:04:12.935
a bursa reduces friction; the joint capsule holds the bones together;

81
00:04:12.935 --> 00:04:17.154
a tendon joins muscle to bone; bone is a lever that articulates;

82
00:04:17.154 --> 00:04:23.085
hyaline cartilage reduces friction and absorbs shock; and a ligament holds bones together.

83
00:04:23.085 --> 00:04:25.392
Synovial joints are freely movable.

84
00:04:25.392 --> 00:04:30.994
The shoulder and hip are ball-and-socket; the knee and elbow are hinges, like a door.

85
00:04:30.994 --> 00:04:36.201
Bursitis is an inflamed, swollen bursa, the friction cushion, and it's painful.
